        <body>
            <div xml:id="d631">

                <pb facs="0644" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg638"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.20/57: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General in Martinique (Malige) to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fort-de-France</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-06-01T17:00:00-04:00">June 1, 1942—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:02 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>203. Department’s No. 115, May 31. From Reber. In conversation with Robert today
                    I explained the Department’s position as set forth in telegram under reference.
                    He appreciates necessity for early conclusion of negotiations and since arrival
                    of preliminary instructions embodied in his “provisional reply” has continued to
                    press <pb facs="0645" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/> Vichy for a definite answer as
                    well as authority for early departure <hi rendition="#italic">Sagittaire</hi>.
                    He has been informed answer will be sent as soon as Laval returns to Vichy
                    tomorrow.</p>
                <p>I have sent Atherton by airmail this morning a copy of Robert’s memo<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d631-f1"/> together with comments. Admiral Hoover has also been
                    furnished copies. [Reber.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Malige</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
